      "scores": {"math_substance": 1, "concreteness": 3, "level_fit": 2, "clarity": 4, "human_voice": 3, "volume": 1, "layout": 4, "readiness": 2},
      "edits": [
        {"page": 1, "problem": "P1", "category": "FORMAT", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Turn them to fit, with no gaps or overlaps.", "fix": "State the activity-wide rule once as a brief unnumbered line at the top of page 1 (use the small blocks, turn them any way, cover the whole shape with no gaps or overlaps), then delete it here and drop the repeated word \"small\" from every problem."},
        {"page": 2, "problem": "P2", "category": "SLOP", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Headings \"Hexagon\", \"Diamond\", \"First filling\", \"Another filling\" above the four outlines", "fix": "Delete all four headings. The problem text already says to fill each shape two ways, and the outlines speak for themselves."},
        {"page": 2, "problem": "P2", "category": "CLARITY", "minutes": 1, "quote": "Ask an adult to trace your pieces before you lift them to use again.", "fix": "Delete. Each shape has two outlines and there are plenty of blocks, so children can leave both fillings in place. If a record is wanted, tracing or a photo goes in the adult notes."},
        {"page": 2, "problem": "P2", "category": "LEVEL", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Fill each shape two different ways. You can use different kinds of small pieces.", "fix": "A single yellow block fills the hexagon, so the task is over in seconds and has no ceiling. Rewrite with a real goal, for example: fill the hexagon in as many different ways as you can, and find the filling with the most pieces and the one with the fewest. Do the same for the diamond, which can take a hexagon plus two triangles."},
        {"page": 3, "problem": "P3", "category": "SLOP", "minutes": 1, "quote": "Turn the pieces as needed. Use them again for the next shape.", "fix": "Delete. Turning is covered by the shared rule. Only five chevrons are needed in all (2 for the arrow, 3 for the star), so there is no reason to reuse them."},
        {"page": 4, "problem": "P4/P5", "category": "SLOP", "minutes": 1, "quote": "Bold labels \"Long hexagon\" and \"Mountain\" above single-shape problems", "fix": "Delete both labels. Each problem has only one shape, and its text already names it."},
        {"page": 4, "problem": "P4", "category": "LEVEL", "minutes": 10, "quote": "Fill the long hexagon using only small blue rhombi. Then fill it again with any small pieces you like.", "fix": "The second sentence has no goal. Replace it with: find three different ways to fill the long hexagon with blue rhombi. Exactly three exist, which gives K-1 an entry point to the 4-5 hexagon tilings. Draw three actual-size outlines."},
        {"page": 4, "problem": "P5", "category": "LEVEL", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Fill the mountain using only small green triangles. Then fill it again with any small pieces you like.", "fix": "Covering a 9-space triangle with 9 greens is rote. Ask instead: can you fill the mountain with blue rhombi, and how few spaces can you leave empty? (The best is 3 blues with 3 spaces empty, the K-1 entry point to the 2-3 work.) Then ask for a filling with the fewest pieces (3 red trapezoids)."},
        {"page": 4, "problem": "end", "category": "VOLUME", "minutes": 30, "quote": "Packet ends after Problem 5, about 15 minutes for a quick child", "fix": "Add about 15 minutes of work: a page of actual-size boards (2-edge triangle, long hexagon, 2-by-2 diamond, 3-edge triangle, a small arrow) where children sort which ones blue rhombi can fill and which always leave a triangle uncovered."},
        {"page": 4, "problem": "end", "category": "VOLUME", "minutes": 30, "quote": "No work for the second half of the 40 minutes", "fix": "Add a second block, for example: build the 2x upscale hexagon or triangle from small pieces with the fewest pieces you can, or count how many green triangles cover the 2x and 3x triangles (4 and 9) and predict the next one."}
      ],
      "summary": "Five actual-size fill-the-outline puzzles. All are drawn correctly and all can be solved (the star takes 3 chevrons, the long hexagon 5 blues), but they are routine for K-1 and touch neither kernel from the outline. The \"then fill it again with any pieces you like\" add-ons have no goal, and a quick child is done in about fifteen minutes. The problems need real goals (all three blue fillings, can blues fill the mountain, fewest pieces), fewer headings and repeated rules, and about 25 more minutes of work."
    },
    "grades-2-3": {
      "pages": 4,
      "problems": 3,
      "minutes_quick_child": 30,
      "minutes_typical_child": 40,
      "scores": {"math_substance": 4, "concreteness": 4, "level_fit": 4, "clarity": 3, "human_voice": 3, "volume": 3, "layout": 3, "readiness": 2},
      "edits": [
        {"page": 1, "problem": "rules", "category": "FORMAT", "minutes": 3, "quote": "\"Keep pieces inside the board without overlaps.\" (P1); \"without overlaps or pieces outside\" (P2); \"Keep pieces inside the triangle without overlaps.\" (P2, page 3); \"Cover whole triangle spaces without overlaps or pieces outside.\" (P3)", "fix": "State once, as a brief unnumbered rule at the top of page 1: pieces cover whole triangle spaces, do not overlap, and stay inside the board. Delete the restatements from every problem."},
        {"page": 1, "problem": "P1", "category": "MATH", "minutes": 10, "quote": "Boards A (3-edge triangle, 9 spaces) and C (11 spaces) cannot be filled; B (12) and D (10) fill easily", "fix": "Both impossible boards have an odd number of spaces, so \"odd number\" fully answers the question and the up/down count is never needed. Board A also repeats the 3-edge triangle from Problem 2. Replace Board A with an even-area board that blues cannot fill, such as a 10-space board with 6 up-triangles and 4 down-triangles, so the four boards contrast."},
        {"page": 1, "problem": "P1", "category": "SLOP", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Which boards can you fill and which ones did you get stuck on? What is stopping you from completely filling some of the shapes?", "fix": "Make it one direct question: \"Which boards can be filled? For each one that cannot, explain why no one could fill it.\""},
        {"page": 2, "problem": "P2", "category": "SLOP", "minutes": 1, "quote": "Record how many blues fit and how many triangle spaces stay empty.", "fix": "Delete. The Blues/Empty spaces blanks under each triangle already say this."},
        {"page": 2, "problem": "P2", "category": "SLOP", "minutes": 1, "quote": "\"2 small edges on each side\", \"3 small edges on each side\", \"4 small edges on each side\", \"5 small edges on each side\"", "fix": "The grid already shows each size, so the prose labels repeat the diagram. Delete them, or replace them with short letters if the children need names to refer to."},
        {"page": 3, "problem": "P2", "category": "FORMAT", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Problem 2: Fit as many small blue rhombi as you can in this triangle. Keep pieces inside the triangle without overlaps. Record your best packing.", "fix": "Page 3 repeats the \"Problem 2:\" label and restates the task already given on page 2 (\"these triangles and the triangle on the next page\"). Delete this paragraph and let the 5-edge triangle simply continue Problem 2."},
        {"page": 3, "problem": "P2", "category": "SLOP", "minutes": 1, "quote": "Compare your four best packings. What do you notice about the number of empty spaces? Explain why you cannot leave fewer.", "fix": "Cut the generic compare/notice step and keep the central question: \"How many spaces stay empty on each triangle? Explain why no one can leave fewer.\" (Answers: 2, 3, 4, 5.)"},
        {"page": 4, "problem": "P3", "category": "CLARITY", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Fewest empty triangle spaces: / Blues: ____ Purples: ____ (under each of A-F)", "fix": "In Problem 2, \"Blues:\" meant the number of pieces. Here it means empty spaces left by blues, so children will write piece counts. Relabel as \"Empty spaces with blues: ___ with purples: ___\". (Answers, blues/purples: A 0/0, B 0/4, C 0/0, D 0/2, E 1/1, F 2/4.)"},
        {"page": 4, "problem": "P3", "category": "SLOP", "minutes": 1, "quote": "Record your best results.", "fix": "Delete. The blanks show what to record."},
        {"page": 4, "problem": "P3", "category": "SCAFFOLDING", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Can you cover one purple exactly with blues? How does this explain which pieces can leave fewer gaps?", "fix": "The first question hands over the key step of the argument, and \"gaps\" switches terminology from \"empty spaces\". Replace with: \"Is there any board where purples leave fewer empty spaces than blues? Find one or explain why not.\" Keep the two-blues-make-a-purple hint in the adult notes."},
        {"page": 4, "problem": "P3", "category": "LAYOUT", "minutes": 3, "quote": "A single answer line directly above the footer for the P3 explanation", "fix": "Tighten the spacing between the three board rows (the boards stay at actual size) to free 3-4 writing lines, or move the question to the top of a new page with room to write."},
        {"page": 4, "problem": "end", "category": "VOLUME", "minutes": 30, "quote": "Packet ends after Problem 3, about 30 minutes for a quick third grader", "fix": "Add a substantial final problem on a large actual-size triangle grid, for example: design a board with an even number of spaces that blues cannot fill, or a board where purples must leave more empty spaces than blues. Then predict, and explain, how many spaces stay empty on a 10-edge triangle."}
      ],
      "summary": "A sound kernel-1 sequence: possible and impossible boards, fewest-gap packings of triangles with 2 to 5 edges (2, 3, 4 and 5 spaces empty), and blues against chevrons on six well-chosen actual-size boards. All diagrams and expected answers check out. Problem 1's two impossible boards both have odd area, so the colouring idea is not yet needed. Rules are restated in every problem, the Problem 3 blanks are ambiguous, its last question gives away the key step, and a quick child runs out after about 30 minutes."
    },
    "grades-4-5": {
      "pages": 5,
      "problems": 10,
      "minutes_quick_child": 38,
      "minutes_typical_child": 55,
      "scores": {"math_substance": 4, "concreteness": 5, "level_fit": 4, "clarity": 4, "human_voice": 3, "volume": 4, "layout": 4, "readiness": 3},
      "edits": [
        {"page": 1, "problem": "P1", "category": "CLARITY", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Find as many different tilings as you can. ... How many different tilings did you find?", "fix": "State the real goal and when the child is done: \"Find all the tilings. How do you know there are no more?\" There are 6. This also gives the later ribbon work its payoff."},
        {"page": 1, "problem": "P1", "category": "CLARITY", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Record each blue rhombus with one line joining the centers of its two trian-gle spaces. (with sample rhombus)", "fix": "This center-to-center notation is never used again: every later card draws rhombus outlines, so children must translate their records. Have them record by drawing the rhombus outlines on the small grids, and delete the box and sample. The badly hyphenated \"trian-gle\" goes with it."},
        {"page": 1, "problem": "P1", "category": "LAYOUT", "minutes": 1, "quote": "\"Keep the bold edge at the bottom\": the bottom edge is only slightly heavier than the other outline edges", "fix": "Orientation decides which tilings count as different and where ribbons start, so make the bottom edge clearly thicker (or colored) on the large board, the small boards and every card."},
        {"page": 2, "problem": "P2", "category": "SCAFFOLDING", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Make a map on these six cards. (cards A-F show all six tilings)", "fix": "The page right after Problem 1 prints every tiling and announces there are six, which answers Problem 1. Add an adult note to hand out pages 2-5 only after Problem 1 is finished, and have children match their own records to the cards."},
        {"page": 2, "problem": "P2", "category": "SCAFFOLDING", "minutes": 1, "quote": "Try the flip shown from A to B, then flip back.", "fix": "Delete \"then flip back\". The diagram and the definition are enough, and the whole-group demonstration covers trying it out."},
        {"page": 2, "problem": "P2", "category": "SCAFFOLDING", "minutes": 1, "quote": "Check each line with your blocks.", "fix": "Delete. This is a directed step the adult can prompt if needed."},
        {"page": 2, "problem": "P2", "category": "SLOP", "minutes": 1, "quote": "Can you get from every card to every other card by a sequence of flips?", "fix": "The answer can be read off the finished six-card map at a glance, so this is a filler follow-up. Delete it or fold it into the map problem below."},
        {"page": 3, "problem": "P3", "category": "SCAFFOLDING", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Problem 3: Find the shortest route from A to F. Write the cards you visit and count your flips. Route: A ____ Number of flips: ____", "fix": "This is a two-minute read-off of the map (A-B-C-E-F or A-B-D-E-F, 4 flips), and the answer format is printed. Merge it with Problem 4 into one problem about the map: fewest flips from A to F, and whether any round trip from A can have an odd number of flips."},
        {"page": 3, "problem": "P4", "category": "SLOP", "minutes": 1, "quote": "Explain what you notice.", "fix": "Replace with \"Explain why.\" (No odd round trip exists: the map is bipartite.)"},
        {"page": 3, "problem": "P5", "category": "SLOP", "minutes": 1, "quote": "At any step, is there a choice of which rhombus to cross next? Could one tiling have two different ribbons starting at the bold edge?", "fix": "Both questions have the same answer, so keep only the second."},
        {"page": 4, "problem": "P6", "category": "SCAFFOLDING", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Problem 6: Draw the ribbon on each of these tilings. Write its code from bottom to top.", "fix": "This repeats Problem 5 four more times on reprinted copies of cards C-F. Fold it into Problem 5 (\"draw the ribbon and write the code on every card\") rather than giving it a separate number. (Codes: A RRLL, B RLRL, C LRRL, D RLLR, E LRLR, F LLRR.)"},
        {"page": 4, "problem": "P7", "category": "SCAFFOLDING", "minutes": 3, "quote": "How many steps does each ribbon take? How many are L? How many are R? Use the shape of the board to explain why.", "fix": "Replace the chain of small directed questions with one: \"What do all six codes have in common? Use the shape of the board to explain why.\""},
        {"page": 4, "problem": "P7", "category": "SCAFFOLDING", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Write every different order you can make with two L’s and two R’s. Match each order to a tiling card. (six Code/Card blanks)", "fix": "This prints the answer to the question just above (two L's and two R's) and the count (six blanks), and it is an unnumbered second task. Replace it with a numbered problem with open space: \"Use ribbon codes to prove Problem 1 has no tilings you missed.\""},
        {"page": 5, "problem": "P8", "category": "SCAFFOLDING", "minutes": 3, "quote": "Draw the four blue rhombi crossed by the ribbon, then fill the remaining spaces with blue rhombi.", "fix": "Remove the method: \"Draw a tiling that has this ribbon. Can a ribbon belong to two different tilings?\" (The answer is no; the ribbons shown are RLLR = card D and LLRR = card F.)"},
        {"page": 5, "problem": "P9/P10", "category": "SCAFFOLDING", "minutes": 10, "quote": "Problem 9: Compare the ribbon codes before and after each flip on your map. What changes? Write three examples. ... Problem 10: Circle every neighboring RL or LR in your six codes. Can you make a flip at every circled pair? Are there any flips that do not come from one of these pairs? Check with the cards and blocks, then describe your rule.", "fix": "Problem 10 prints the intended discovery (a flip swaps one neighboring LR pair) as a procedure, and neither problem returns to the odd-round-trip question of Problem 4, which is the payoff of kernel 2. Merge them into one problem: \"What does one flip do to a ribbon code? Use this to explain your answer to Problem 4.\" Give it a full workspace."}
      ],
      "summary": "Mathematically accurate and carefully drawn. The large board is the 1-2-2 hexagon at actual size, the six cards are exactly its six tilings (A RRLL, B RLRL, C LRRL, D RLLR, E LRLR, F LLRR), the flip example matches cards A and B, and every printed ribbon is correct. However, page 2 shows all six tilings right after Problem 1 asks children to find them. Problems 5-10 also break the ribbon discovery into small directed steps that print the answers (two L's and two R's, circle the LR pairs) and never return to explain Problem 4."
    }
  },
  "overall": {
    "readiness": 2,
    "strongest": "Grades 4-5 carries a deep kernel-2 sequence on a correctly drawn actual-size board with exact tiling cards and correct ribbons; it mainly needs de-scaffolding and the six cards held back until Problem 1 is done.",
    "weakest": "K-1 is a short run of routine fill-the-outline puzzles that connects to neither kernel and runs out after about fifteen minutes of a forty-minute block."
  }
